            <div xml:id="d349">

                <pb facs="0713" n="681" xml:id="pg681"/>

                <head>349. Memorandum From Marshall Wright of the National Security Council Staff to
                    the President’s Special Assistant (Rostow)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d349-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1968-03-25">March 25, 1968</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item> SEACOORD Recommendations<anchor n="2" xml:id="d349-f2"/>
                    </item>
                </list>
                <p>The SEACOORD recommendations seem to me to be based on the assumption that we can
                    take additional military actions in Laos, and that otherwise the situation stays
                    the same.</p>
                <p>That assumption is almost certainly incorrect. The enemy will certainly take
                    steps to counter our ploy. They will attempt to destroy our forces introduced on
                    to their supply line. And they may very possibly use our action as a pretext for
                    an assault upon major Laotian cities.</p>
                <p>In short, if we “hot up” the war in Laos we can expect with confidence that the
                    enemy will do the same for he has consistently shown fewer inhibitions in Laos
                    than we have shown. And he has the resources on the spot. Unless we are prepared
                    to contemplate a major war in Laos with the overt commitment of American
                    resources we had better be very careful, indeed, in deciding on the SEACOORD
                    recommendations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Marshall Wright</hi>
                        <anchor n="3" xml:id="d349-f3"/>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
